,title,link,text,language,family
0,Athabaskan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Athabaskan_languages,"Athabaskan, also known as Dene, is a large branch of the Na-Dene language family of North America, located in western North America in three areal language groups: Northern, Pacific Coast and Southern.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
1,Tsetsaut - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tsetsaut,"The Tsetsaut (Nisga'a language: Jits'aawit; in the Tsetsaut language: Wetaŀ or Wetaɬ) were an Athabaskan -speaking group whose territory was around the head of the Portland Canal, straddling what is now the boundary between the US state of Alaska and the Canadian province of British Columbia.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
2,Langues athapascanes — Wikipédia,https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Langues_athapascanes,"Le terme athapascan ou athabascan[1], ainsi que le terme déné, désigne un vaste ensemble de peuples amérindiens et la famille de langues qu’ils parlent. Ces peuples sont principalement répartis en deux grandes zones géographiques en Amérique du Nord : le Sud-Ouest et le Nord-Ouest.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
3,Eyak Athabaskan Crossword Clue - Sporcle,https://www.sporcle.com/reference/clue/eyak-athabaskan,"For the word puzzle clue of eyak athabaskan, the Sporcle Puzzle Library found the following results. Explore more crossword clues and answers by clicking on the results or quizzes.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
4,"Alaska Natives -- Aleuts, Tanana, Eyak, Tlingit, YupikAthabaskan languages - WikipediaTsetsaut - WikipediaNA-DENE LANGUAGES - YouTubeThe Tsetsaut Language - ydli.org",https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YvSV7UYrg_E,"Jul 31, 2013 · Indigenous northern music (jew´s harp / jaw´s harp). Alaskan Indians / natives are indigenous peoples of Alaska: Inupiaq, Yupik, Aleut, Eyak, Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian, and a number of... Because both Tlingit and Eyak are fairly remote from the Athabaskan languages in terms of their sound systems, comparison is usually done between them and the reconstructed Proto-Athabaskan language. The Tsetsaut (Nisga'a language: Jits'aawit; in the Tsetsaut language: Wetaŀ or Wetaɬ) were an Athabaskan -speaking group whose territory was around the head of the Portland Canal, straddling what is now the boundary between the US state of Alaska and the Canadian province of British Columbia. Jul 19, 2023 · Welcome to my channel! This is Andy from I love languages. Let's learn different languages/dialects together. *Some of the pronunciations may not be accurate... The name Tsetsaut comes from /ts'əts'aut/, a term meaning ""those of the interior"" used by the Gitksan and Nisga'a to refer to the Athabaskan-speaking people to the north and east of them, including not only the Tsetsaut but some Tahltan and Sekani.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
5,NA-DENE LANGUAGES - YouTubeThe Tsetsaut Language - ydli.org,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JL0uH_XbzEs,"Jul 19, 2023 · Welcome to my channel! This is Andy from I love languages. Let's learn different languages/dialects together. *Some of the pronunciations may not be accurate... The name Tsetsaut comes from /ts'əts'aut/, a term meaning ""those of the interior"" used by the Gitksan and Nisga'a to refer to the Athabaskan-speaking people to the north and east of them, including not only the Tsetsaut but some Tahltan and Sekani.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
6,The Tsetsaut Language - ydli.org,http://ydli.org/langs/tsetsaut.htm,"The name Tsetsaut comes from /ts'əts'aut/, a term meaning ""those of the interior"" used by the Gitksan and Nisga'a to refer to the Athabaskan-speaking people to the north and east of them, including not only the Tsetsaut but some Tahltan and Sekani.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
7,Tsetsaut | Ethnologue Free,https://www.ethnologue.com/language/txc/,Tsetsaut is a dormant language of Canada and the United States. It belongs to the Eyak-Athabaskan language family.,Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
8,Athabaskan-Eyak-Tlingit Languages - Sorosoro Sorosoro,https://www.sorosoro.org/en/athabaskan-eyat-tlingit-languages/,"Tsetsaut: extinct. Pacific Coast Athabaskan Sub-family. Athabaskan Oregon Branch.As for the isolated languages, Tsetsaut, all of the Kwalhioqua-Clatskanie dialects, and Eyak are now extinct.",Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
9,The Athabaskan Language Family - Native American Netroots,https://nativeamericannetroots.net/diary/Indians-101-The-Athabaskan-Language-Family,The Athabaskan (also spelled Athapaskan and Athabascan) language family is found in the western American Indian culture areas. Linguists feel that the Athabaskan language family is one branch of a larger genetic grouping called Athabaskan-Eyak.,Tsetsaut,Eyak-Athabaskan
